Michael and Lendl, Bernhard and Leuchters, Michael and Lewis, Evan and Lieb, Edward and Lloyd, Gloria and Losek, Angelika and Lu, Yao and Maestracci, Saadia and Mangan, Dennis and Mares, Alberto W. and Barnett, Juan Mazar and McClain, Valerie and McNair, John Sydney and Michael, Terry and Miller, Lloyd and Monzani, Partizia and Moran, Belen and Morris, Mike and Mößmer, Georg and Mountain, Johny and Phuthe, Onnie Mary Moyo and Muñoz, Marcos and Nakken, Sheri and Wambui, Anne Nduta and Neunteufl, Bettina and Nikolić, Dimitrije and Oberoi, Devesh V. and Obmode, Gregory and Ogar, Laura and Ohara, Jo and Rybine, Naion Olej and Owen, Bryan and Owen, Kim Wilson and Parikh, Rakesh and Pearce, Nicholas J. G. and Pemmer, Bernhard and Piper, Chris and Prince, Ian and Reid, Terence and Rindermann, Heiner and Risch, Stefan and Robbins, Josh and Roberts, Seth and Romero, Ajeandro and Rothe, Michael Thaddäus and Ruiz, Sergio and Sacher, Juliane and Sackl, Wolfgang and Salletmaier, Markus and Sanand, Jairaj and Sauerzopf, Clemens and Schwarzgruber, Thomas and Scott, David and Seegers, Laura and Seppi, David and Shields, Kyle and Siller-Matula, Jolanta and Singh, Beldeu and Sithole, Sibusio and Six, Florian and Skoyles, John R. and Slofstra, Jildou and Sole, Daphne Anne and Sommer, Werner F. and Sonko, Mels and Starr-Casanova, Chrislie J. and Steakley, Marjorie Elizabeth and Steinhauser, Wolfgang and Steinhoff, Konstantin and Sterba, Johannes H. and Steppan, Martin and Stindl, Reinhard and Stokely, Joe and Stokely, Karri and St-Pierre, Gilles and Stratford, James and Streli, Christina and Stryg, Carl and Sullivan, Mike and Summhammer, Johann and Tadesse, Amhayes and Tavares, David and Thompson, Laura and Tomlinson, Alison and Tozer, Jack and Trevisanato, Siro I. and Trimmel, Michaela and Turner, Nicole and Vahur, Paul and family=Byl, given=Jennie, prefix=van der, useprefix=true and family=Maas, given=Tine, prefix=van der, useprefix=true and Varela, Leo and Vega, Carlos A. and Vermaak, Shiloh and Villasenor, Alex and Vogel, Matt and family=Wintzigerode, given=Georg, prefix=von, useprefix=true and Wagner, Christoph and Weinberger, Manuel and Weinberger, Peter and Wilson, Nick and Wolfe, Jennifer Finocchio and Woodley, Michael A. and Young, Ian and Zuraw, Glenn and Zwiren, Nicole},
  date = {2012-10-01},
  journaltitle = {Theoretical Medicine and Bioethics},
  shortjournal = {Theor Med Bioeth},
  volume = {33},
  number = {5},
  pages = {359--376},
  issn = {1573-1200},
  doi = {10.1007/s11017-012-9233-1},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1007/s11017-012-9233-1},
  urldate = {2021-03-03},
  abstract = {Peer review is a widely accepted instrument for raising the quality of science. Peer review limits the enormous unstructured influx of information and the sheer amount of dubious data, which in its absence would plunge science into chaos. In particular, peer review offers the benefit of eliminating papers that suffer from poor craftsmanship or methodological shortcomings, especially in the experimental sciences. However, we believe that peer review is not always appropriate for the evaluation of controversial hypothetical science. We argue that the process of peer review can be prone to bias towards ideas that affirm the prior convictions of reviewers and against innovation and radical new ideas. Innovative hypotheses are thus highly vulnerable to being “filtered out” or made to accord with conventional wisdom by the peer review process. Consequently, having introduced peer review, the Elsevier journal Medical Hypotheses may be unable to continue its tradition as a radical journal allowing discussion of improbable or unconventional ideas. Hence we conclude by asking the publisher to consider re-introducing the system of editorial review to Medical Hypotheses.},
  langid = {english}
}

